00:03:19.289 --> 00:03:19.956
Hi, everyone.

00:03:20.015 --> 00:03:24.308
I'm AmyJune Hineline, and today I
want to talk about accessibility a

00:03:24.309 --> 00:03:26.251
little differently than I usually do.

00:03:26.882 --> 00:03:31.870
A lot of accessibility work happens
after content, um, already exists.

00:03:31.898 --> 00:03:36.835
Uh, we review it, we find problems, we fix
it, and then often, um, find the same kind

00:03:36.835 --> 00:03:38.891
of problems again, but somewhere else.

00:03:39.194 --> 00:03:41.850
Um, so this talk is about moving
some of that work earlier.

00:03:42.251 --> 00:03:47.102
Instead of asking, "How do we fix
this?" after the fact, what we would,

00:03:47.259 --> 00:03:51.417
if we could, make accessibility
decisions before the content is created.

00:03:51.524 --> 00:03:53.406
And this is content specifically.

00:03:53.495 --> 00:03:57.779
Um, and this is where style guides
can really become useful, not just

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.812
as editorial references, but as a way
to make accessible choices part of

00:04:01.814 --> 00:04:03.488
how a team works from the beginning.

00:04:04.863 --> 00:04:06.322
So a little bit of housekeeping.

00:04:06.325 --> 00:04:08.045
There are some images in the deck.

00:04:08.327 --> 00:04:11.802
Most of them are decorative, so
I'm not going to explain them, but

00:04:11.815 --> 00:04:16.166
if, if they enhance the content,
