00:04:24.495 --> 00:04:26.477
and community, um, for having me back.

00:04:26.477 --> 00:04:29.076
I really appreciate
being able to, to speak.

00:04:30.506 --> 00:04:35.341
So I am the certification community
architect for the global skills

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.203
team at the Linux Foundation.

00:04:37.221 --> 00:04:42.859
I support the education's global team
in helping them develop and maintain

00:04:43.158 --> 00:04:46.908
exam and training content, and the
related documentation across the

00:04:46.912 --> 00:04:49.483
foundation's, uh, global skills portfolio.

00:04:49.517 --> 00:04:52.015
I help organize a few Drupal events.

00:04:52.043 --> 00:04:56.027
I'm a member of the Drupal community
working group conflict resolution team.

00:04:56.366 --> 00:05:02.121
Um, I am a hospice nurse by
trade, so I am in touch with,

00:05:02.196 --> 00:05:04.222
uh, many end user challenges.

00:05:04.269 --> 00:05:08.357
And then I help organize a meetup called
A11y Talks where we get together once

00:05:08.357 --> 00:05:10.090
a month and talk about accessibility.

00:05:11.342 --> 00:05:12.733
So here's where we're headed today.

00:05:12.851 --> 00:05:16.823
We'll talk about some of the,
those small content decisions

00:05:16.828 --> 00:05:20.284
that can create accessibility
problems, you know, the patterns.

00:05:20.626 --> 00:05:26.744
Um, we'll… Things like headings,
links, instructions, terminology, images.

00:05:27.082 --> 00:05:30.277
None of them necessarily feel
like huge decisions when we make

00:05:30.278 --> 00:05:34.293
them, but all of these choices
add up as, and have an end result.

00:05:34.525 --> 00:05:38.357
Then we'll look at what happens when
we stop relying on every individual

00:05:38.466 --> 00:05:42.379
contributor to have to remember
every accessibility requirement,

00:05:42.391 --> 00:05:46.342
and start building better defaults
into the way that we create content.

00:05:46.599 --> 00:05:48.495
We'll also look at what
useful accessibility

00:05:48.496 --> 00:05:50.034
guidance actually looks like.

00:05:50.453 --> 00:05:53.971
I just wanna start with five decisions
that are worth documenting, and

00:05:53.972 --> 00:05:58.001
ways to put that guidance closer to
where people who are doing the work.

00:05:58.287 --> 00:06:02.169
Then we'll talk about keeping a style
guide useful over time, and most

00:06:02.171 --> 00:06:05.413
impor- importantly how to start small.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.765
You don't need to document every
accessibility requirement, um,

00:06:08.765 --> 00:06:10.105
before this becomes useful.

00:06:12.191 --> 00:06:16.020
So like I said, accessibility
issues generally start small.

00:06:16.441 --> 00:06:20.989
Um, these are the kinds of acce- ac- these
are some of the issues that start small.

00:06:21.046 --> 00:06:23.607
A heading level can get skipped
because someone likes the way

00:06:23.974 --> 00:06:25.274
a different heading looks.

00:06:25.832 --> 00:06:30.518
A, a link gets labeled click here
because the destination feels

00:06:30.585 --> 00:06:32.633
obvious when it's set in context.

00:06:32.931 --> 00:06:36.418
An instruction tells someone to
choose the option on the right.

00:06:36.506 --> 00:06:39.630
An image gets alt text, but the
description does not actually

00:06:39.631 --> 00:06:41.441
communicate, um, what the

00:06:41.871 --> 00:06:43.174
why the image is there.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:46.905
Again, none of these decisions
necessarily feel significant in the

00:06:46.907 --> 00:06:51.697
moment, but when the same pattern
gets repeated across pages, across

00:06:51.697 --> 00:06:55.690
lessons, across documentation, or
across entire digital assets and

00:06:55.691 --> 00:07:00.543
websites, um, those decisions start
creating larger accessibility problems.

00:07:00.991 --> 00:07:04.287
This is the part what- that
makes the work a little tricky.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:08.126
Um, we're not always dealing
with one dramatic failure.

00:07:08.174 --> 00:07:11.734
Often we're dealing with, uh,
lots of ordinary content decisions

00:07:11.758 --> 00:07:14.484
that we, that we repeat over time

00:07:17.498 --> 00:07:20.981
And also this is where those small
decisions start getting expensive if you

00:07:20.981 --> 00:07:23.028
wanna look at it from a cost analysis.

00:07:23.559 --> 00:07:27.509
Um, if we catch an issue while someone
is creating the content, it may take a

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:32.055
few seconds to fix, but if we catch it
later, that same pattern might already

00:07:32.056 --> 00:07:34.525
exist in dozens or hundreds of places.

00:07:34.555 --> 00:07:37.704
Um, you know, we, we work in
content management systems where the

00:07:37.719 --> 00:07:42.739
content is displayed dynamically,
so one, one, one entry can be

00:07:42.739 --> 00:07:44.763
across d- multiple spaces, right?

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:48.958
Um, the content may have gone
through review and approval, you

00:07:48.959 --> 00:07:50.353
know, so that costs us some money.

00:07:50.565 --> 00:07:54.795
Other contributors may have also copied
the same pattern because they assumed

00:07:55.018 --> 00:07:56.861
it was the preferred way to do things.

00:07:57.077 --> 00:08:01.050
So now fixing it means finding it
in every instance, um, making the

00:08:01.051 --> 00:08:05.371
correction, and potentially putting the
content through another round of review.

00:08:06.358 --> 00:08:09.985
So remediation is not just about the
time it takes to fix one accessibility

00:08:09.985 --> 00:08:14.448
issue, it's a- also about all of the
work that grows around the issue once

00:08:14.448 --> 00:08:16.361
it becomes part of the normal workflow.

00:08:16.847 --> 00:08:23.384
The longer we allow a problematic
pattern to h- to remain in use or keep

00:08:23.384 --> 00:08:28.054
using it, the more o- opportunities
we, uh, create for it to spread.

00:08:30.302 --> 00:08:32.368
This is where it became
really concrete for me.

00:08:32.413 --> 00:08:37.931
In my work, um, with certification
exams and training content, I

00:08:37.932 --> 00:08:42.283
kept seeing the same accessibility
issues come back again and again.

00:08:42.828 --> 00:08:46.410
Reviewers would fix them, but then the
same kinds of problems would appear in

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:50.141
new content because the people creating
that content did not always have an

00:08:50.141 --> 00:08:53.887
example or a documented pattern to follow.

00:08:54.677 --> 00:08:56.826
And that is an important distinction.

00:08:56.908 --> 00:09:01.195
Um, the problem was not that the
contributors did not care about

00:09:01.195 --> 00:09:04.827
accessibility, the problem was that
we were asking people to make the same

00:09:04.827 --> 00:09:10.639
accessibility decisions over and over
without giving them enough guidance.

00:09:10.665 --> 00:09:15.082
Um, once we started documenting these
decisions in a style guide, we could

00:09:15.082 --> 00:09:20.391
move, uh, a lot of that remediation and
review work, um, earlier in the process.

00:09:20.576 --> 00:09:24.442
So instead of correcting the same issue
repeatedly, we could point to that

00:09:24.442 --> 00:09:26.441
preferred pattern from the beginning.

00:09:26.921 --> 00:09:33.903
And this is why repeated corrections can
be used as a, uh, a useful signal as well.

00:09:34.176 --> 00:09:38.183
They often tell us that the issue
is not just one person making a

00:09:38.183 --> 00:09:42.644
mistake, it m- may mean that the
workflow is missing a good default.

00:09:44.334 --> 00:09:48.189
And those better defaults really
make the accessibility choice easier,

00:09:48.481 --> 00:09:50.914
um, 'cause it makes it consistently.

00:09:50.914 --> 00:09:54.132
You know, accessibility
by memory does not scale.

00:09:54.423 --> 00:09:56.797
Better defaults, not better memory.

00:09:59.183 --> 00:10:04.710
So a useful style guide reduces the
time, reduces the need to make the same

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:06.870
accessibility decision from scratch.

00:10:08.556 --> 00:10:12.977
If the same accessibility
decision comes up again and again,

00:10:13.382 --> 00:10:17.158
contributors should not have to stop
every time, search for guidance,

00:10:17.244 --> 00:10:18.992
interpret it, and decide what to do.

00:10:19.411 --> 00:10:23.745
We can make that decision once, document
that preferred approach, and give

00:10:23.766 --> 00:10:26.525
people an example that they can reuse.

00:10:26.911 --> 00:10:30.299
An example is a really
important part of a style guide.

00:10:30.828 --> 00:10:34.777
Um, this does not remove the need
for accessibility knowledges, but it

00:10:34.778 --> 00:10:39.094
reduces the number of routine decisions
people have to make from scratch.

00:10:39.374 --> 00:10:44.451
And when that preferred pattern is easy
to find in one place, a style guide, um,

00:10:44.634 --> 00:10:50.270
we are much less likely to be recreating
that same accessibility issue or problem.

00:10:50.614 --> 00:10:56.443
In this image, um, it's, uh, an example
of a color guide for the Linux Foundation.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:58.259
Um, uh-

00:11:00.357 --> 00:11:03.842
Uh, it, it, it defines some of
the patterns that we use for

00:11:03.842 --> 00:11:05.101
branding and that kind of thing.

00:11:07.195 --> 00:11:12.868
So the goal is to make that decision
once, so instead of asking every

00:11:12.869 --> 00:11:16.306
contributor to solve the same problem
again, and they might do it differently.

00:11:16.606 --> 00:11:21.757
So start by identifying the accessible
choice, then we document it, and

00:11:21.757 --> 00:11:25.179
then we show what it looks like in
practice, and what the guidance-

00:11:25.438 --> 00:11:30.145
and make that guidance available
whenever the same decision comes up.

00:11:30.525 --> 00:11:32.592
The value is in reuse.

00:11:32.655 --> 00:11:36.949
Um, if we already know that pattern,
that preferred way to write a link,

00:11:37.204 --> 00:11:40.939
structure a heading, or describe an
image, that answer should not live in one

00:11:40.939 --> 00:11:43.791
person's head or in one review comment.

00:11:43.944 --> 00:11:48.255
It should bec-come part of that shared
workflow so the next curs- person,

00:11:48.269 --> 00:11:52.511
the next content creator or designer
can make the same decision early.

00:11:54.367 --> 00:11:58.566
Um, so I've designed a few
style guides in my history.

00:11:58.568 --> 00:12:02.969
Um, I worked at opensource.com and we
had writers, we curated articles, and

00:12:02.969 --> 00:12:06.676
once we defined the style guide, we
had far less, um, revisions to make.

00:12:06.964 --> 00:12:10.492
And then, um, when I started at the
Linux Foundation, we didn't have a style

00:12:10.493 --> 00:12:12.258
guide, and so now we've implemented that.

00:12:12.762 --> 00:12:17.942
Um, because people always have the
same questions, um, and a useful style

00:12:17.944 --> 00:12:22.204
guide should answer those questions,
answer the questions that contributors

00:12:22.212 --> 00:12:23.708
are likely to have in that moment.

00:12:23.729 --> 00:12:27.225
The more you work with people, the more
patterns you recognize in these questions.

00:12:27.519 --> 00:12:30.853
Um, a lot of them are like,
"What am I supposed to do?

00:12:31.133 --> 00:12:32.156
Why does it matter?

00:12:32.329 --> 00:12:34.709
What does the preferred pattern look like?

00:12:34.748 --> 00:12:40.074
What should I avoid?" And if something
needs to change, who owns that guidance?

00:12:40.075 --> 00:12:41.420
Who owns that style guide?

00:12:41.946 --> 00:12:44.442
So the important part is that
contributors should not have to

00:12:44.459 --> 00:12:48.875
interpret a vague rule and figure
out what it means for themselves.

00:12:49.285 --> 00:12:54.395
That clear guidance really reduces, um,
mental fatigue during content creation.

00:12:54.577 --> 00:12:55.982
It reduces the guesswork.

00:12:56.170 --> 00:13:00.119
Um, it gives people something concrete
that they can recognize and apply.

00:13:02.204 --> 00:13:04.954
So we talked about this
kind of basic structure.

00:13:04.955 --> 00:13:07.790
Look- let's look at what that
guidance should look like.

00:13:08.322 --> 00:13:14.388
Usable accessibility guidance should
be specific enough that someone can

00:13:14.437 --> 00:13:18.875
act on it without having to stop
and interpret what the rule means.

00:13:18.906 --> 00:13:21.133
I know I say this a lot, but
this is an important part.

00:13:21.469 --> 00:13:26.492
It should tell contributors what to do,
give them a reason, and show them an

00:13:26.493 --> 00:13:28.962
example they can recognize and reuse.

00:13:33.626 --> 00:13:37.362
The goal is not to create a
giant accessibility manual.

00:13:37.534 --> 00:13:40.365
The goal is to make
everyday decisions easier.

00:13:41.054 --> 00:13:45.136
Here's an example of what that structure
can look like in a style guide.

00:13:45.476 --> 00:13:46.951
The rule is specific.

00:13:48.099 --> 00:13:51.742
We write link text that
identifies the destination or

00:13:51.786 --> 00:13:54.405
action, then we explain why.

00:13:55.283 --> 00:13:59.321
A link should still make sense
when someone encounters it

00:13:59.347 --> 00:14:01.268
outside the surrounding sentence.

00:14:02.006 --> 00:14:05.601
Then we show the pattern
we want them to use.

00:14:06.921 --> 00:14:11.137
You know, read the accessibility
report, and we show what to avoid.

00:14:11.201 --> 00:14:13.125
We want to avoid click here.

00:14:13.743 --> 00:14:19.333
Finally, you know, um, we identify who
owns that guidance because accessibility

00:14:19.333 --> 00:14:24.212
guidance needs to be, uh… needs someone
responsible for maintaining it over time.

00:14:24.219 --> 00:14:26.706
You know, if you've worked in
accessibility for any amount of time,

00:14:26.706 --> 00:14:30.229
you know that it's constantly changing,
and this never, never has an end point.

00:14:30.229 --> 00:14:31.785
It's always that, that target.

00:14:31.843 --> 00:14:33.046
The target's always moving.

00:14:33.504 --> 00:14:37.817
So the important thing is this does
not leave the contributor guessing.

00:14:37.977 --> 00:14:42.102
It gives them a rule, the reason, and
an example they can walk away with.

00:14:44.135 --> 00:14:48.288
Rules should help people make a
decision, not remind them what

00:14:48.288 --> 00:14:50.659
accessi- that accessibility matters.

00:14:50.988 --> 00:14:56.992
Use accessible headings sounds good,
but often the con- it leaves someone

00:14:57.008 --> 00:14:59.860
asking, "Okay, so what does that mean?"

00:15:00.307 --> 00:15:06.652
Um, so choose heading levels based on
content structure, not visual appearance,

00:15:06.864 --> 00:15:09.757
gives them something concrete to do.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:13.854
That is the difference between a
principle and a usable guidance.

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:19.059
A good rule should really help someone
make the right choice while they are

00:15:19.059 --> 00:15:23.803
working without requiring them to
stop and translate the guidance first.

00:15:24.112 --> 00:15:28.130
And examples are far more useful
and faster to understand than

00:15:28.136 --> 00:15:30.053
paragraphs of explanation.

00:15:32.434 --> 00:15:36.670
There's a lot of accessibility
decisions a team could document,

00:15:37.134 --> 00:15:42.496
but these five are a strong place
to start because they often…

00:15:43.627 --> 00:15:47.685
They come up a lot in our everyday
content, um, creation in our work.

00:15:47.913 --> 00:15:54.149
We'll look at semantic structure, s-
meaningful links, clear instructions,

00:15:54.536 --> 00:15:59.348
consistent plain language, which,
you know, if you're working with a

00:15:59.351 --> 00:16:03.950
global audience, which most of us are
these days because, um, the internet

00:16:03.962 --> 00:16:08.187
has made the, the world a lot more
reachable, um, could mean using

00:16:08.187 --> 00:16:11.086
global English or, um, translations.

00:16:11.436 --> 00:16:14.929
And then we'll talk about images
and alternative text as well.

00:16:15.877 --> 00:16:21.168
So the goal is not to cover every possible
accessibility requirement, it's to focus

00:16:21.199 --> 00:16:25.806
on repeatable content decisions where
a little guidance can prevent the same

00:16:25.807 --> 00:16:28.087
problem from showing up again and again.

00:16:28.994 --> 00:16:32.798
So headings do more than
make content look organized.

00:16:32.818 --> 00:16:36.476
Um, they create that structure that people
use to understand and navigate the page.

00:16:36.726 --> 00:16:41.244
So the guidance around that should
be clear about using descriptive

00:16:41.246 --> 00:16:47.773
headings and keeping that, um, that,
that, that, uh, that level logical.

00:16:47.806 --> 00:16:50.549
You know, the heading level should
reflect the relationship between

00:16:50.551 --> 00:16:54.696
sections, not how large or bold
someone wants the text to appear.

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:58.995
If something needs to look
different, that's a styling decision.

00:16:58.997 --> 00:17:02.529
You know, we use CSS or
the design system for that.

00:17:03.197 --> 00:17:08.775
Um, keeping structure and appearance, uh,
separate helps preserve that predictable

00:17:08.776 --> 00:17:12.887
hierarchy, which makes the content
easier to navigate and understand.

00:17:14.788 --> 00:17:16.866
Here's a simple before and after example.

00:17:17.166 --> 00:17:21.562
In the first version, we have an H1 for
a course title, and then we jump all

00:17:21.562 --> 00:17:23.975
the way down to H4 for getting started.

00:17:24.027 --> 00:17:26.661
After that, requirements
is just bold text.

00:17:27.424 --> 00:17:33.469
Visually, someone could style all, all
three of these, um, so they look perfectly

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:37.097
reasonable on the page, but structurally,
they're not telling the same story.

00:17:37.341 --> 00:17:40.872
Um, you know, especially for people
who are, um, navigating your content,

00:17:41.184 --> 00:17:43.690
um, using assistive technology.

00:17:45.197 --> 00:17:49.163
The heading level should reflect the
relationship between the sections.

00:17:49.165 --> 00:17:53.369
So getting started should follow the
course title as an H2, and requirements

00:17:53.371 --> 00:17:55.450
should be nested beneath it as an H3.

00:17:55.803 --> 00:17:57.929
This is why visual
appearance is not enough.

00:17:57.938 --> 00:18:01.864
The two pages can look identical
and still have enough, um, uh, very

00:18:01.864 --> 00:18:03.473
different underlying structures.

00:18:07.818 --> 00:18:09.568
So the second, um-

00:18:11.837 --> 00:18:16.452
The second thing worth, like, putting
into your style guide are, um, is

00:18:16.452 --> 00:18:18.274
the concept of meaningful links.

00:18:18.588 --> 00:18:23.468
Um, for links, that main question
is whether the text makes the

00:18:23.468 --> 00:18:25.918
destination or the action clear.

00:18:26.373 --> 00:18:30.867
Um, when assistive technology, um,
accesses links, sometimes they'll do it

00:18:30.867 --> 00:18:34.231
on a rotor so all they, all they read
are the links, so they need to make

00:18:34.316 --> 00:18:39.096
sense out of, uh, out of, out of the
context of what's e- else is on the page.

00:18:39.447 --> 00:18:44.164
So, you know, most of us know that
click here and learn more, um, make,

00:18:44.549 --> 00:18:47.916
may make sense when someone reads the
full sentence, but the link itself does

00:18:47.916 --> 00:18:50.412
not provide the information we need.

00:18:50.687 --> 00:18:53.761
So a better pattern is to make that
link text meaningful on its own.

00:18:54.024 --> 00:18:56.639
Tell people what they are opening,
what they are reading, what they are

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:58.187
downloading or what they're starting.

00:18:58.540 --> 00:19:04.596
It's also helpful to indicate when the
destination is something different, um,

00:19:04.706 --> 00:19:10.590
maybe it's a PDF or it's another file
type, uh, so there's fewer surprises.

00:19:10.808 --> 00:19:16.183
And while I mention PDF, um, working,
um… This is a talk I can do for

00:19:16.208 --> 00:19:19.203
hours some, some other time where
you can ask me questions about it.

00:19:19.569 --> 00:19:21.790
Um, we shouldn't be using PDFs anymore.

00:19:21.797 --> 00:19:26.503
They're an antiquated way of accessing
information and inherently inaccessible.

00:19:26.706 --> 00:19:30.263
So, um, but that, like I said,
that's another talk for another day.

00:19:30.655 --> 00:19:35.678
Um, so with this meaningful link text,
it's the goal is to remove the guesswork.

00:19:35.851 --> 00:19:40.351
If more specific link text is
available, we need to use it.

00:19:41.804 --> 00:19:43.508
So here's what it looks like in practice.

00:19:43.909 --> 00:19:48.228
Each of these examples, the surrounding
sentence gives us some content-- context,

00:19:48.562 --> 00:19:52.216
but the actual link text is pretty vague.

00:19:52.418 --> 00:19:55.738
You know, click here, here, or just link.

00:19:56.142 --> 00:20:00.277
Um, I see this a lot in, in our
newsletters from the Linux Foundation

00:20:00.279 --> 00:20:02.401
where it, it just says link at the end.

00:20:02.864 --> 00:20:07.543
Um, this means that the reader has
to rely on the sentence around it

00:20:07.544 --> 00:20:09.101
to understand what the link does.

00:20:09.112 --> 00:20:12.274
You know, a stronger version puts the
meaning, um, into the link itself.

00:20:12.277 --> 00:20:16.101
So instead of click here, use
something like review the s- review

00:20:16.101 --> 00:20:17.805
the certification requirements.

00:20:18.043 --> 00:20:23.080
Instead of use this link to
register, um, change it to

00:20:23.081 --> 00:20:24.935
register for the training workshop.

00:20:25.474 --> 00:20:30.739
The goal is for the link text itself to
communicate that action it's performing

00:20:30.739 --> 00:20:35.523
or the destination so that people, um,
do not have to piece that information

00:20:35.523 --> 00:20:37.459
together with the surrounding content.

00:20:38.679 --> 00:20:39.786
So clear instructions.

00:20:39.786 --> 00:20:42.832
These are really important in
a, in a lot of our, in a lot of

00:20:42.833 --> 00:20:44.989
our everyday, uh, activities.

00:20:45.411 --> 00:20:45.914
Um-

00:20:50.717 --> 00:20:54.174
This is another place where
accessibility can improve just by

00:20:54.180 --> 00:21:01.910
making the language more precise, um,
using direct, familiar wording and

00:21:01.922 --> 00:21:06.374
name controls by the labels people
actually encounter in the interface.

00:21:06.712 --> 00:21:11.420
Um, if there are multiple steps, present
them in the order that they happen.

00:21:11.779 --> 00:21:14.634
Uh, that reduces the amount of
interpretation someone has to do

00:21:14.634 --> 00:21:16.638
while, uh, trying to complete the task.

00:21:16.932 --> 00:21:22.328
So avoid relying on visual descriptions
like location, shape, color, or direction.

00:21:22.572 --> 00:21:26.014
You know, a good test is whether
the instruction could still make

00:21:26.016 --> 00:21:28.230
sense if the layout changed.

00:21:28.810 --> 00:21:33.507
Um, if it does not, that wording
is probably depending too much

00:21:33.508 --> 00:21:35.034
on the visual presentation.

00:21:35.905 --> 00:21:39.331
And remember, everyone accesses
information differently, so the way

00:21:39.331 --> 00:21:43.889
I access a visual asset, you know,
something that's, uh, you know, uh,

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.074
silver and shiny might be distracting
to me and put me on a different

00:21:47.189 --> 00:21:48.780
place on the page than someone else.

00:21:50.645 --> 00:21:54.133
Another set of examples where the
instruction looks too much what… on-

00:21:54.191 --> 00:21:59.746
relies too much on, um, what the interface
looks like, uh, use the button below.

00:22:00.185 --> 00:22:04.358
This assumes that the layout will always
place the button below the text, you know.

00:22:04.464 --> 00:22:06.997
Choose the green option on the right.

00:22:07.338 --> 00:22:10.421
That's not only position,
but it's color as well.

00:22:11.008 --> 00:22:16.130
Uh, click the icon in the upper
left corner, um, assumes someone can

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:20.433
identify that control visually and that
it will stay in that same location.

00:22:20.591 --> 00:22:25.872
You know, we all work with mobile and
iPads and, and laptops and big screens.

00:22:25.874 --> 00:22:29.718
That button is not always gonna be
in the same place depending on your,

00:22:29.766 --> 00:22:31.776
on, on your, on your breakpoints.

00:22:32.199 --> 00:22:38.565
Um, a stronger instruction names
the control and action directly.

00:22:38.884 --> 00:22:41.933
Instead of describing where
something is or what it looks

00:22:41.934 --> 00:22:44.062
like, tell people what to select.

00:22:44.464 --> 00:22:50.492
That makes the instruction more
reliable across the various

00:22:50.492 --> 00:22:54.300
layouts that we might have across
those devices and the different

00:22:54.301 --> 00:22:56.475
ways of navigating the interface.

00:22:58.827 --> 00:23:01.778
So instructions should use
the same language people

00:23:01.827 --> 00:23:03.615
encounter in the interface.

00:23:03.951 --> 00:23:10.360
If the control says save
draft, call it save draft.

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:15.773
If the action is called publish, use
publish consistently instead of switching

00:23:15.774 --> 00:23:19.192
between publish, submit or finish.

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:25.072
That consistency matters because changing
terminology can make people wonder-

00:23:28.156 --> 00:23:32.245
It can make people wonder whether
we are talking about the same thing.

00:23:32.737 --> 00:23:38.615
If it has multiple actions, break them
up into separate steps rather than

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:41.514
packing everything into one sentence.

00:23:41.796 --> 00:23:46.839
Um, I have a tip on this slide
that says, "Do not make readers

00:23:46.852 --> 00:23:50.361
interpret an expression before
they can complete the task."

00:23:54.561 --> 00:23:56.238
Plain language.

00:23:56.333 --> 00:23:59.963
I think, um, plain language is underrated.

00:24:00.285 --> 00:24:08.624
Um, it's less about making everything
sound simple and more about reducing

00:24:08.754 --> 00:24:11.362
unnecessary effort for the reader.

00:24:12.805 --> 00:24:17.487
This happens a lot in our training
and certification content where people

00:24:17.487 --> 00:24:22.154
become fatigued because they have
to l- use a dictionary of, uh, every

00:24:22.155 --> 00:24:25.265
other paragraph or maybe, you know,
English isn't their first language.

00:24:25.468 --> 00:24:32.188
They have to look up the instruction when
if we would just used a, a, a, a, a plain

00:24:32.188 --> 00:24:34.886
word, um, we wouldn't get as fatigued.

00:24:36.625 --> 00:24:40.840
So consistency is one of the easiest
ways to make content easier to process,

00:24:40.860 --> 00:24:42.870
you know, using those familiar words.

00:24:43.213 --> 00:24:47.404
Put the main point first and keep
sentences focused so readers do

00:24:47.405 --> 00:24:50.456
not have to work through extra
wording to find what matters.

00:24:50.759 --> 00:24:55.589
Um, we all know this, you know,
people don't always read anymore,

00:24:55.589 --> 00:24:57.159
especially when they're on their phones.

00:24:57.328 --> 00:24:59.128
They scroll, right?

00:25:00.141 --> 00:25:03.940
Um, so reinforce that main idea first.

00:25:04.312 --> 00:25:08.576
Um, if every time the wording
changes, readers have to decide

00:25:08.576 --> 00:25:10.689
whether the meaning changed too.

00:25:11.391 --> 00:25:16.593
Consistent language reduces
the extra interpretation and

00:25:16.797 --> 00:25:20.677
helps people move through that
content with way less friction.

00:25:21.485 --> 00:25:29.715
So sign in, log in, access
your content, enter the portal.

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:36.254
These might mean all the same thing,
but changing the language makes your

00:25:36.254 --> 00:25:42.209
user or your reader stop and wonder
if that action has changed as well.

00:25:42.482 --> 00:25:46.298
So once you choose a
term, use it consistently.

00:25:46.463 --> 00:25:50.337
That predictable language reduces
that unnecessary cognitive

00:25:50.338 --> 00:25:52.055
load that can lead to fatigue.

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:57.655
When we introduced global English
into our style guides, we started

00:25:57.655 --> 00:26:03.037
seeing fewer customer service tickets
that were really about ambiguity.

00:26:03.391 --> 00:26:07.750
Um, people were not necessarily having
technical issues with our content.

00:26:07.787 --> 00:26:12.892
Um, they were contacting support
because a question or instruction

00:26:12.976 --> 00:26:15.625
could be interpreted more than one way.

00:26:15.932 --> 00:26:17.021
And I'm gonna say that again.

00:26:19.917 --> 00:26:23.389
They didn't know how to answer
questions or follow the training

00:26:23.399 --> 00:26:28.615
because a question or instruction
could be interpreted more than one way.

00:26:28.916 --> 00:26:33.038
So once we started, like, really
standardizing the terminology,

00:26:33.248 --> 00:26:38.194
rem- re- we removed idioms and
localisms and made the questions more

00:26:38.204 --> 00:26:43.030
direct, those what does this mean
ticket, those counts went way down.

00:26:44.303 --> 00:26:47.692
So that's another cost eff- effective
thing about having style guides

00:26:47.693 --> 00:26:52.276
and using, you know, this global
language, um, concept is, uh,

00:26:52.576 --> 00:26:54.575
less customer support was needed.

00:26:55.822 --> 00:27:00.629
And that, um, it's really a useful
for- for reminder for me when the

00:27:00.629 --> 00:27:03.800
tickets started going down that
that plain language is not just

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:06.568
about making content nicer to read.

00:27:07.291 --> 00:27:12.063
Uh, clearer content can reduce support
needs because fewer people have to

00:27:12.063 --> 00:27:14.205
stop and ask someone to interpret it.

00:27:17.485 --> 00:27:18.395
I love examples.

00:27:18.513 --> 00:27:21.805
Um, this is plain language, you
know, about being about clarity,

00:27:22.082 --> 00:27:23.761
not just using shorter words.

00:27:24.148 --> 00:27:29.162
Piece of cake may be familiar, that
expression piece of cake may be familiar

00:27:29.162 --> 00:27:31.497
to some people, but it's an idiom.

00:27:32.065 --> 00:27:35.609
Someone has to understand the
ex- expression before they can

00:27:35.650 --> 00:27:37.331
understand the instruction.

00:27:37.701 --> 00:27:42.121
Um, close of business sounds
very precise, but is it?

00:27:42.483 --> 00:27:43.990
Um, whose business day?

00:27:44.185 --> 00:27:45.891
What time zone, you know?

00:27:46.149 --> 00:27:48.143
Um, what time exactly?

00:27:48.276 --> 00:27:52.690
Uh, my end of business day
is 5:00 PM Pacific Time.

00:27:53.917 --> 00:27:55.721
N- not for someone in India.

00:27:56.709 --> 00:27:59.338
So these revised versions
say exactly what we mean.

00:27:59.948 --> 00:28:05.320
Um, follow these steps to complete
the process, and submit the

00:28:05.322 --> 00:28:08.216
form by 5:00 PM Eastern Time.

00:28:10.974 --> 00:28:16.309
We're removing the need for extra
interpre- interpretation and

00:28:16.323 --> 00:28:20.494
giving people the information they
actually need to complete a task,

00:28:20.667 --> 00:28:22.585
and to complete a task on time.

00:28:24.351 --> 00:28:27.063
So the fifth pattern
is images and alt text.

00:28:27.076 --> 00:28:31.117
Um, I've given some really fun
presentations on alternative text.

00:28:31.368 --> 00:28:35.875
Um, if you wanna look those up, they're,
they're really, uh, great examples that

00:28:35.876 --> 00:28:41.700
are fun, um, uh, uh, if you wanna learn
more about, uh, meaningful alt text.

00:28:43.625 --> 00:28:47.968
For images, the guidance needs
to cover more than simply whether

00:28:47.968 --> 00:28:50.466
an alt text field gets filled in.

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:54.741
Um, someone needs to decide whether
the image actually needs alternative

00:28:54.741 --> 00:28:58.947
text, whether it is decorative,
and what information that image is

00:28:58.947 --> 00:29:01.218
contributing to that particular context.

00:29:01.239 --> 00:29:05.184
And I think when we talk about repeated
content or we're working with, you

00:29:05.185 --> 00:29:07.711
know, different teams, we're working
with product teams, we're working with

00:29:07.725 --> 00:29:14.904
customer service, we're working with,
um, marketing, um, we all interpret

00:29:14.905 --> 00:29:16.803
why that image is, is valuable.

00:29:19.361 --> 00:29:21.503
But it's also clear to talk
about the responsibility.

00:29:21.505 --> 00:29:24.075
You know, who understands
why the image is being used?

00:29:24.297 --> 00:29:26.873
Who writes the alternative
text, and who reviews it?

00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:31.510
And if the image changes later, who makes
sure the alt text still makes sense?

00:29:31.885 --> 00:29:36.359
That ownership matters because
good alt text depends on purpose.

00:29:36.736 --> 00:29:39.403
It's not about technical field
that someone can complete at

00:29:39.403 --> 00:29:43.144
the end of a, uh, at the end of
the content creation process.

00:29:45.765 --> 00:29:50.985
Alt text is about communicating what the
image is doing in that particular context.

00:29:51.251 --> 00:29:56.313
The exact same image can serve different
purposes in different places, which means

00:29:56.315 --> 00:30:00.788
that the useful alt text may change too,
and documenting this is really important.

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:05.182
Sometimes that information
is, is what is pictured.

00:30:05.595 --> 00:30:09.624
Sometimes what's important about it
is the action that's being performed,

00:30:10.125 --> 00:30:15.587
or, um, a detail, or a piece of
information that the image supports.

00:30:16.195 --> 00:30:21.295
And sometimes that the image is decorative
and does not need alternative text at all.

00:30:21.618 --> 00:30:27.033
So rather than asking what is this
a picture of, a more useful question

00:30:27.033 --> 00:30:29.254
is why is this image even here?

00:30:29.464 --> 00:30:31.851
Or what does someone need to know from it?

00:30:32.272 --> 00:30:35.396
Um, I have a little exercise
to show what this means.

00:30:37.975 --> 00:30:45.234
So when we say the word, the, the
combination of words dead bug, what,

00:30:45.424 --> 00:30:47.762
what may that conjure up in your brain?

00:30:49.485 --> 00:30:53.180
So here's a picture of a dead bug.

00:30:55.327 --> 00:30:59.752
And here's a different
picture of a dead bug.

00:31:00.402 --> 00:31:04.269
Very different images but the
same alternative text, right?

00:31:05.594 --> 00:31:09.207
So we wanna be more specific
about explaining that image.

00:31:09.437 --> 00:31:16.494
Um, so that first dead bug is… I
might use this alt text instead, "An

00:31:16.553 --> 00:31:21.455
assassin bug on a green leaf carrying
a stack of dead bugs on its back."

00:31:21.665 --> 00:31:24.046
Well, that really defines
what that dead bug is.

00:31:24.114 --> 00:31:29.037
And then the ne- the next one is, "A
vintage rusted out Volkswagen Beetle

00:31:29.050 --> 00:31:35.312
sitting under a tree with Grateful Dead
logos on its body." Now we know that

00:31:35.312 --> 00:31:39.387
those are two very different images,
but they're both images of dead bugs.

00:31:45.736 --> 00:31:48.708
A style guide is useful, but we
can take this a step further.

00:31:49.014 --> 00:31:54.650
If contributors have to stop what they
are doing, leave the editor, search

00:31:54.650 --> 00:31:57.561
through the documentation, and find
the right accessibility rule every

00:31:57.562 --> 00:32:01.576
time they make a decision, we're still
putting a lot of responsibility on them.

00:32:01.815 --> 00:32:05.789
The real opportunity is to bring that
guidance closer to the work itself.

00:32:06.128 --> 00:32:10.231
In WordPress, that can mean letting
the decisions we have documented

00:32:10.233 --> 00:32:15.089
start influencing the CMS itself, the
patterns, the templates, the controls,

00:32:15.267 --> 00:32:17.751
the options contributors already use.

00:32:18.093 --> 00:32:22.395
There's less distance between the
guidance and the moment someone

00:32:22.406 --> 00:32:26.656
needs it, the easier it becomes
to make the accessible choice.

00:32:28.870 --> 00:32:32.500
The closer accessibility guidance is
to the moment someone is making the

00:32:32.500 --> 00:32:35.331
decision, the more useful it becomes.

00:32:35.790 --> 00:32:41.633
That guidance does not have to live only,
to live only in one big style guide.

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:47.620
It can show up in author templates,
editorial checklists, contributor

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:53.485
documentation, examples besides form
fields, reusable content patterns, and

00:32:53.601 --> 00:32:55.925
even in review comments or onboarding.

00:32:59.365 --> 00:33:02.991
The idea really is to meet people
where they are already working.

00:33:05.308 --> 00:33:09.538
If someone has to leave their workflow,
go search for a document, and figure

00:33:09.540 --> 00:33:12.135
out which rule applies, there's
a good chance that they're gonna

00:33:12.146 --> 00:33:14.133
skip the step or not get it right.

00:33:14.499 --> 00:33:18.452
Um, but if that guidance is already there,
right when the decision is being made,

00:33:18.467 --> 00:33:21.134
it becomes easier to use consistently.

00:33:23.439 --> 00:33:29.329
Requirements tell people what they
are supposed to do, but examples show

00:33:29.329 --> 00:33:31.566
them what that actually looks like.

00:33:33.316 --> 00:33:37.572
For each rule, I like to include
a preferred example, something

00:33:37.572 --> 00:33:41.584
to avoid, and a short explanation
of why that difference matters.

00:33:42.208 --> 00:33:46.289
And when the topic needs more detail,
the style guide can link out to a

00:33:46.302 --> 00:33:50.495
deeper guidance rather than trying
to explain everything in one place.

00:33:50.665 --> 00:33:53.998
The goal is not to overwhelm our
contributors or content creators

00:33:54.845 --> 00:33:56.536
with every possible edge case.

00:33:57.007 --> 00:33:59.792
It's to show them what success
looks like so they have something

00:33:59.794 --> 00:34:01.899
concrete they can recognize and apply.

00:34:03.027 --> 00:34:07.025
The goal is not to turn every
contributor into an accessibility expert.

00:34:08.817 --> 00:34:12.668
For everyday content decisions, the
guidance should help people recognize

00:34:12.668 --> 00:34:15.704
that a decision is being made,
show them that preferred pattern,

00:34:15.725 --> 00:34:18.425
and make it clear when something
falls outside that usual guidance.

00:34:18.724 --> 00:34:20.308
That last part really matters.

00:34:20.689 --> 00:34:26.212
A style guide should not pretend to answer
every possible accessibility question.

00:34:26.524 --> 00:34:32.253
It should help contributors handle
the common cases confidently and

00:34:32.253 --> 00:34:36.690
recognize when they need to bring
in someone with deeper e- expertise.

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:40.761
That way, we support different people
at different experience levels without

00:34:40.772 --> 00:34:42.671
lowering the accessibility standard.

00:34:43.564 --> 00:34:48.343
And once we know what the preferred
accessible patterns are, we can start

00:34:48.343 --> 00:34:49.977
building them into WordPress itself.

00:34:50.365 --> 00:34:54.513
That might mean creating approved block
patterns, defining heading strucker-

00:34:54.575 --> 00:34:58.637
structures, providing accessible
button styles, limiting color choices,

00:34:58.698 --> 00:35:02.398
this is an important one, limiting
color choices, requiring labels where

00:35:02.398 --> 00:35:08.391
possible, or giving our, our creators and
contributors reusable content templates.

00:35:08.612 --> 00:35:11.457
The idea is not to remove that
flexibility that everyone enjoys.

00:35:11.593 --> 00:35:16.825
It's just makes it easier to make the
safe choice … makes it easier for,

00:35:16.885 --> 00:35:18.555
for us to find that safer choice.

00:35:19.157 --> 00:35:22.558
If the option is already built into
our authoring experiences, contributors

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:25.615
don't have to remember the rule and
recreate the solution every time.

00:35:25.916 --> 00:35:30.186
The tools and the templates can carry
some of that responsibility for us.

00:35:32.383 --> 00:35:34.160
And these two pieces work together.

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:36.429
You know, the style guide
e- explains the decision.

00:35:36.466 --> 00:35:39.759
It tells contributors what the
preferred pattern is, why it

00:35:39.761 --> 00:35:41.038
matters, and what it looks like.

00:35:41.374 --> 00:35:44.738
So if the guide says we have an approved
button pattern, WordPress can make

00:35:44.738 --> 00:35:47.071
that button or that pattern available.

00:35:47.317 --> 00:35:49.949
If the guide defines how
headings should be structured,

00:35:49.949 --> 00:35:51.410
the templates can reinforce.

00:35:51.879 --> 00:35:55.396
Documentation gives people the
knowledge, and good defaults

00:35:55.411 --> 00:35:56.966
reduce the effort required.

00:35:57.163 --> 00:35:57.945
Nobody--

00:36:00.071 --> 00:36:03.787
No one has to carry that entire
accessibility process on their own.

00:36:04.944 --> 00:36:08.415
So we really need to, do need
to keep the guide usable.

00:36:08.482 --> 00:36:09.580
So start small.

00:36:09.790 --> 00:36:13.259
A style guide does not have to
cover every accessibility edge case.

00:36:13.261 --> 00:36:14.223
We talked about that.

00:36:14.707 --> 00:36:17.703
Um, begin with the
problems you see repeated.

00:36:18.274 --> 00:36:21.721
These are usually the best candidates
for shared guidance because you already

00:36:21.721 --> 00:36:23.502
know they are creating extra work.

00:36:23.884 --> 00:36:25.893
Uh, make the guidance easy to search.

00:36:25.926 --> 00:36:27.173
Use direct language.

00:36:27.174 --> 00:36:31.506
Include examples so contributors can
quickly recognize that preferred pattern.

00:36:31.848 --> 00:36:35.350
The guide itself should model the
same accessibility and clarity

00:36:35.366 --> 00:36:39.774
patterns, practices we are
asking contributors to follow.

00:36:40.725 --> 00:36:42.502
It also needs clear ownership.

00:36:42.712 --> 00:36:46.837
Someone should be responsible for
maintaining it, reviewing the guidance,

00:36:46.837 --> 00:36:49.558
and deciding how, um, updates get made.

00:36:49.879 --> 00:36:53.959
Contributors should know how to suggest
changes when they notice something that

00:36:53.959 --> 00:36:59.274
is unclear or has become outdated and no
longer matches, uh, the way the CMS works.

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:02.806
So examples, um, also need
to be revisited over time.

00:37:02.857 --> 00:37:07.087
Interfaces change, patterns change,
and guidance, uh, that was once

00:37:07.093 --> 00:37:10.067
correct, um, may stop being useful.

00:37:10.287 --> 00:37:13.189
So without ownership, that
style guide can slowly become

00:37:13.189 --> 00:37:14.578
a collection of old decisions.

00:37:14.585 --> 00:37:17.584
With ownership, it can stay
relevant and continue supporting

00:37:17.585 --> 00:37:18.837
the people who rely on it.

00:37:19.455 --> 00:37:23.812
So the practical takeaway is, um, you
don't need to build the entire style

00:37:23.814 --> 00:37:25.176
guide before you can make progress.

00:37:25.337 --> 00:37:28.692
Pick one accessibility issue that
keeps showing up in your reviews.

00:37:28.962 --> 00:37:32.444
Make the decision about what
the preferred pattern should be.

00:37:32.682 --> 00:37:34.424
Write the rule clearly.

00:37:34.765 --> 00:37:39.278
Add an example so people c- can
see what success looks like, and

00:37:39.279 --> 00:37:42.402
put that guidance somewhere where
contributors will actually find it.

00:37:44.064 --> 00:37:45.850
And give someone responsibility.

00:37:45.888 --> 00:37:50.469
That one rule may feel small, but it's
already doing what we have talked about

00:37:50.469 --> 00:37:55.819
through this entire session, moving
an accessibility decision earlier,

00:37:55.835 --> 00:37:59.214
making it reusable, and reducing
the chance that it happens again.

00:38:00.183 --> 00:38:02.156
And that's really the
outcome we're aiming for.

00:38:02.408 --> 00:38:07.002
Um, accessibility works better when
it's not treated as an extra step that

00:38:07.006 --> 00:38:08.908
someone has to remember at the end.

00:38:09.356 --> 00:38:14.593
If the preferred patterns are documented,
built into the workflow, supported by

00:38:14.598 --> 00:38:19.638
the tools people use, then the accessible
choice becomes the normal choice.

00:38:19.817 --> 00:38:23.370
It stops feeling like a
special accessibility task.

00:38:23.598 --> 00:38:27.201
It just becomes how the
team creates content.

00:38:28.977 --> 00:38:33.687
So thank you very much for your time,
and thanks again to the WordPress

00:38:33.692 --> 00:38:35.500
accessibility team for having me back.

00:38:35.555 --> 00:38:38.265
There's a few links on here
where you can find me across

00:38:38.404 --> 00:38:39.627
some of the social networks.

00:38:39.627 --> 00:38:42.604
I'm usually Volkswagen
Chick in most of the spaces.

00:38:43.490 --> 00:38:43.991
Thank you.

00:38:49.919 --> 00:38:51.528
Wow, that was incredible, AmyJune.

00:38:51.718 --> 00:38:52.588
Thank you so much.

00:38:52.592 --> 00:38:56.163
I love, love, love style sheets,
uh, so I really appreciate

00:38:56.164 --> 00:38:58.433
that from a design perspective.

00:38:58.639 --> 00:39:00.628
Uh, we have quite a few
questions in the chat.

00:39:01.361 --> 00:39:02.295
Um, let's get to 'em.

00:39:04.111 --> 00:39:04.356
All right.

00:39:04.356 --> 00:39:08.325
What is your opinion us- on using
AI to help draft up a style guide?

00:39:09.635 --> 00:39:10.121
Okay.

00:39:10.122 --> 00:39:15.978
So, um, I work for the Linux Foundation,
so this is an unofficial opinion on AI.

00:39:16.343 --> 00:39:21.180
Um, I don't believe in using AI, but I
know that it's inevitable and people will.

00:39:21.532 --> 00:39:26.986
So I think that AI can, um,
be really useful for getting

00:39:26.988 --> 00:39:28.373
that style guide started.

00:39:28.402 --> 00:39:31.315
Um, it can help you,
like, organize your ideas.

00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:35.749
It can perhaps even, uh, suggest
structure and maybe even draft

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:37.171
some examples really quickly.

00:39:38.288 --> 00:39:43.996
But, right, I would not treat
that output as authoritative.

00:39:44.096 --> 00:39:48.336
Um, accessibility guidance still
needs that human in the loop.

00:39:48.396 --> 00:39:53.757
Um, we need to review for the accuracy,
the content, the context, and, um,

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:56.991
whether it actually fits for the people
that we're aiming for, you know, and

00:39:56.991 --> 00:40:00.500
the content and the workflows, um,
of the people who are helping us.

00:40:00.778 --> 00:40:05.287
So the blank page is really hard, and
we all know that, so sometimes when

00:40:05.289 --> 00:40:09.799
we have that AI output, that helps
us, like, really destroy it, right?

00:40:09.799 --> 00:40:13.036
You know how it's easy when you already
have something and you can take it apart.

00:40:13.037 --> 00:40:15.476
But it definitely needs a human in a loop.

00:40:16.477 --> 00:40:16.896
Great.

00:40:16.973 --> 00:40:19.970
Um, I've got a couple of PDF
questions, if you don't mind.

00:40:20.262 --> 00:40:20.642
Oh, God.

00:40:20.658 --> 00:40:21.212
Um- Okay.

00:40:21.581 --> 00:40:24.315
What do you recommend as
an alternative to a PDF?

00:40:24.354 --> 00:40:26.279
And then I have a second part
when you get done with that.

00:40:26.477 --> 00:40:27.055
Okay.

00:40:27.426 --> 00:40:27.999
So-

00:40:30.138 --> 00:40:32.599
I'm not gonna vote for
PDFs in November either.

00:40:32.822 --> 00:40:33.178
Um,

00:40:36.342 --> 00:40:38.895
But, um, usually- That should've
been off the ballot a long time ago.

00:40:39.762 --> 00:40:44.081
But usually an HTML
webpage is a good solution.

00:40:44.084 --> 00:40:44.383
You know?

00:40:44.383 --> 00:40:46.934
There's really no reason
for us to have PDFs anymore.

00:40:47.134 --> 00:40:50.713
We're not p- we're not really printing
things, and the technology that we were

00:40:50.713 --> 00:40:55.580
using those PDFs for definitely has
advanced that they can, like, read, um,

00:40:55.592 --> 00:40:58.083
structured off of a, off of a webpage.

00:40:58.490 --> 00:40:58.985
Um,

00:41:01.006 --> 00:41:06.872
m- mostly I suggest HTML because it's
responsive, it's searchable, it's

00:41:07.069 --> 00:41:12.406
typically accessible across our different
devices and assistive technologies.

00:41:12.747 --> 00:41:17.834
But if a PDF is still needed, I
like offering the same information

00:41:18.048 --> 00:41:22.841
in a web accessible format too,
like a web format, you know, rather

00:41:22.843 --> 00:41:25.707
than making PDF the only option.

00:41:25.719 --> 00:41:28.307
So if you do have them, I
would say give an alternative.

00:41:28.931 --> 00:41:29.174
Right.

00:41:29.310 --> 00:41:30.969
And I think that answered
the second question.

00:41:31.156 --> 00:41:34.542
The second question was what's an
alternative, um, in the EV flow.

00:41:34.654 --> 00:41:36.795
So all right, so let's move
on to the next question.

00:41:37.254 --> 00:41:41.586
Uh, do you address whether to use title
or sentence case in your style guide?

00:41:41.883 --> 00:41:45.282
It's hard to find consistent info
on whether one is accessible.

00:41:45.819 --> 00:41:49.514
Also, what, what about
ampersands and parentheses,

00:41:50.538 --> 00:41:52.281
such as to introduce acronyms?

00:41:53.149 --> 00:41:53.603
Okay.

00:41:53.604 --> 00:41:57.340
So I've… That's a
multiple question for me.

00:41:57.362 --> 00:42:00.531
Maybe a, a two-part quest- or
three-part question for me.

00:42:00.900 --> 00:42:04.996
Um, so let's talk about
sentence case first.

00:42:05.020 --> 00:42:09.469
Um, I usually recommend sentence case
because it's easier to read, and it

00:42:09.471 --> 00:42:14.018
creates a more consistent experience,
especially with headings and buttons and

00:42:14.018 --> 00:42:19.953
labels, but that's really a personal or an
organization or a design choice, you know?

00:42:19.973 --> 00:42:24.642
But for me, I find that, you know, when
we're working with, um, with folks who

00:42:24.642 --> 00:42:28.431
might have, um, cognitive disabilities,
that sentence case is easier for them

00:42:28.432 --> 00:42:32.154
to parse, um, because they can see it
as a sentence and one clear thought.

00:42:32.544 --> 00:42:39.057
Um, for acronyms, um, I do
recommend, um, spelling them out

00:42:39.062 --> 00:42:41.912
the first time and putting that
acronym in parentheses, right?

00:42:42.175 --> 00:42:46.977
Um, like Web Content, uh, Accessibility
Guidelines and then, you know,

00:42:47.105 --> 00:42:50.365
uh, the WCAG in parentheses.

00:42:50.930 --> 00:42:53.346
And then after that, you
know, using it on your own.

00:42:53.626 --> 00:42:59.658
Um, if the acronym is… That's if the,
if, if it's common for your audience.

00:42:59.658 --> 00:43:03.352
You may not need to define it every
time, but definitely that first time

00:43:03.371 --> 00:43:07.335
ev- on every, on every, like, at
the beginning of your webinar or at

00:43:07.336 --> 00:43:08.753
the beginning of your white paper.

00:43:09.103 --> 00:43:14.628
So and then let's talk about ampersands
and all those kinds of signs.

00:43:14.976 --> 00:43:24.790
Um, for screen readers, I think,
and for folks who, um, uh, maybe

00:43:24.790 --> 00:43:27.182
come from a different language set
or something like that, some of

00:43:27.182 --> 00:43:32.703
those characters can be confusing,
so I typically recommend only using

00:43:32.707 --> 00:43:34.939
characters that are on your keyboard.

00:43:35.532 --> 00:43:38.647
If you have to, like, type a
special way to get, like, a…

00:43:39.172 --> 00:43:42.269
I don't like em dashes either
for a lot of different reasons.

00:43:42.270 --> 00:43:47.723
But if you have to type it special
on your keyboard, typically not all

00:43:47.938 --> 00:43:52.325
accessibility or assistive technology
can read that the way you intend it,

00:43:52.331 --> 00:43:53.941
and it can kind of mess things up.

00:43:54.205 --> 00:43:58.893
So I do recommend that you stick to those
characters on your standard keyboard.

00:44:00.069 --> 00:44:00.314
All right.

00:44:00.316 --> 00:44:04.216
And then, and then, um, I didn't
know if you t- touched on the title

00:44:04.216 --> 00:44:05.851
or sentence case in the style guide.

00:44:06.332 --> 00:44:09.118
Yeah, I think that's- Okay
… personal, but mostly I recommend

00:44:09.118 --> 00:44:11.477
the sentence case 'cause it's
easier- Got it … to parse, yeah.

00:44:11.586 --> 00:44:11.800
Yeah.

00:44:11.957 --> 00:44:12.136
Got it.

00:44:12.392 --> 00:44:14.719
All right, so we're, we're back at PDFs.

00:44:15.014 --> 00:44:15.029
Okay.

00:44:15.029 --> 00:44:16.933
Um, they love your stance on PDFs.

00:44:17.273 --> 00:44:20.699
Uh, what are your thoughts on PDF
alternatives on mobile devices?

00:44:21.395 --> 00:44:22.092
Okay.

00:44:22.884 --> 00:44:28.813
So I think my hatred for PDFs
came from accessing them on mobile

00:44:28.813 --> 00:44:31.469
devices, um, to begin with, right?

00:44:31.494 --> 00:44:35.495
And then finding the hatred just
seep out all over, um, after that.

00:44:35.977 --> 00:44:39.282
Uh, but they can be really frustrating.

00:44:39.293 --> 00:44:42.895
You know, I worked in hospice for
a really long time and, you know,

00:44:42.897 --> 00:44:46.507
sometimes our elderly people or people
who had traumatic brain injuries,

00:44:46.516 --> 00:44:48.177
all they had was their mobile device.

00:44:48.602 --> 00:44:51.966
Um, and they found those, the, the
forms that we had at the hospice to

00:44:51.970 --> 00:44:55.471
be especially frustrating because
they required people to, like,

00:44:55.754 --> 00:45:01.116
zoom in and do horizontal scrolling
and really awkward navigation.

00:45:01.445 --> 00:45:06.764
Um, so even a technically
accessible PDF really did not work.

00:45:06.788 --> 00:45:09.825
Well, I don't know if there's a
thing as an accessible PDF, but

00:45:10.110 --> 00:45:12.053
they didn't work on the phone.

00:45:12.080 --> 00:45:14.375
You know, it was just this
frustrating experience,

00:45:14.375 --> 00:45:15.919
especially with those scroll bars.

00:45:16.284 --> 00:45:20.628
So if the content's really meant to
be read on a phone, I'd go back to

00:45:20.628 --> 00:45:26.282
that HTML page, um, and offer the PDF
as an optional download again, so.

00:45:27.872 --> 00:45:28.593
All right, thank you.

00:45:28.593 --> 00:45:28.908
Yeah.

00:45:28.908 --> 00:45:32.933
Uh, what's a convincing argument for
plain language when stakeholders claim

00:45:33.142 --> 00:45:37.741
that their target audience is educated
adults who don't need simpler language?

00:45:37.991 --> 00:45:38.415
Okay.

00:45:38.551 --> 00:45:41.678
So plain language doesn't mean
simple language necessarily.

00:45:41.743 --> 00:45:43.986
Maybe I got that wording wrong in my talk.

00:45:43.986 --> 00:45:44.825
I can't remember.

00:45:45.212 --> 00:45:51.073
Um, so I work in a… I work building
exams for the, the Linux Foundation, and

00:45:51.075 --> 00:45:55.033
we have a global audience, and because
we're a non-profit, we don't always

00:45:55.042 --> 00:45:58.477
have the funds to translate our exams.

00:45:58.714 --> 00:46:02.131
And so when I first started on it,
you know, a number of years ago,

00:46:02.524 --> 00:46:06.086
some of these test questions were,
like, overly complicated, and then

00:46:06.087 --> 00:46:09.861
the- the- the- the choices, you know,
the answers and the distractors,

00:46:10.178 --> 00:46:12.895
were overly, like, verbose, right?

00:46:13.363 --> 00:46:18.328
And we would get support tickets saying
that there was ambiguous language.

00:46:18.350 --> 00:46:22.118
But once we, like, switched to global
English, like a globified, glob-

00:46:22.332 --> 00:46:26.519
global English- Mm-hmm … our support
tickets for that, you know, ambiguous

00:46:26.587 --> 00:46:35.395
wording really went down because
plain language reduces cognitive load.

00:46:36.006 --> 00:46:36.067
I guess so.

00:46:36.069 --> 00:46:41.229
Um, it makes information easier to
understand the first time, right?

00:46:41.493 --> 00:46:44.203
Of course, like, there's white papers,
there's all, you know, all those

00:46:44.216 --> 00:46:47.370
kinds of things, but when we're, like,
talking about our global audience

00:46:47.372 --> 00:46:51.499
and our, and our digital assets being
accessible for- for people, especially

00:46:51.499 --> 00:46:56.423
when they can't be translated, um, you
know, reading in that second language.

00:46:56.453 --> 00:46:59.819
And, you know, even, like, for people
with those episodic disabilities,

00:46:59.819 --> 00:47:03.598
like a migraine at the end of the day
or you've had a full workday, you've

00:47:03.600 --> 00:47:10.428
been on your computer, that cognitive
load may keep people from continuing

00:47:10.428 --> 00:47:12.723
to engage with your content, you know?

00:47:12.982 --> 00:47:16.428
Um, especially when you're trying
to do something quickly, right?

00:47:16.604 --> 00:47:20.397
You know, um, so I really think that
it's… it makes the information

00:47:20.435 --> 00:47:23.473
easier to understand the first
time, to sort of iterate on that.

00:47:24.493 --> 00:47:24.870
Awesome.

00:47:24.870 --> 00:47:25.247
Awesome.

00:47:25.589 --> 00:47:28.147
Um, the next one is an alt text question.

00:47:28.473 --> 00:47:30.841
Do you use alt text for
images behind headings?

00:47:32.509 --> 00:47:36.821
Um, do I use alt text for
images behind headings?

00:47:37.134 --> 00:47:40.950
I'm not quite sure what that means,
but I will- I'm assuming dec-

00:47:40.977 --> 00:47:43.086
decorative, but I- I wanna stay- Okay.

00:47:43.091 --> 00:47:44.247
So, okay … accurate first.

00:47:44.247 --> 00:47:44.257
Okay.

00:47:44.257 --> 00:47:50.437
So, you know, if that alt… if that
image is purely decorative, um, behind the

00:47:50.437 --> 00:47:54.942
heading, I would usually give it an empty
alt text so the screen reader skips it.

00:47:55.348 --> 00:48:00.484
Um, if the image adds information that's
not conveyed by the heading, um, or

00:48:00.500 --> 00:48:03.973
the nearby text, then it definitely
needs that meaningful alt text.

00:48:04.165 --> 00:48:08.618
But also, like, I give a- a… I
don't, I don't know if I did it for-

00:48:08.952 --> 00:48:13.993
for this conference or not, but I give
a talk on, like, providing alt text,

00:48:14.256 --> 00:48:16.189
and I talk about decorative images.

00:48:16.464 --> 00:48:16.942
And-

00:48:19.357 --> 00:48:23.162
Is really think about why you're
using that decorative image.

00:48:23.206 --> 00:48:28.174
Like, if it's conveying,
like, mood or feeling,

00:48:30.848 --> 00:48:35.940
you're doing a disservice to your
content consumer by not describing it.

00:48:36.350 --> 00:48:39.219
So really think about that
